<BaseLayout title="About: Sinapeos" description="Why Sinapeos exists. The psychology behind it. The mission to tell stories of humanity solving hard problems." canonical="/about">
  <article style="line-height: 1.8;">
    <h1 style="font-size: 1.8rem; font-family: var(--font-serif); margin-bottom: 1rem;">Why Sinapeos</h1>

    <p>Negativity sells. It has always sold. The algorithms learned it, the news cycles optimized for it, and our brains (wired for threat detection) cannot look away.</p>

    <p>But here is the thing nobody tells you: <strong>doom sells because it is certain.</strong> "Everything is terrible" is a closed loop. It is comfortable because no action is required. You read it, you feel bad, you scroll.</p>

    <p>This site exists because there is another kind of certainty. The kind that says: <em>"This was terrible. Here is exactly how it got fixed."</em></p>

    <blockquote>Not "everything is fine." But "something was broken, and we fixed it. Here is how."</blockquote>

    <p>Every story on Sinapeos follows the same arc: a problem that looked hopeless, the wrong turns people made trying to solve it, the specific breakthrough that turned things around, and the lesson it leaves us. No toxic positivity. No "everything is getting better" fluff. Just earned hope. Grounded in data, told like a thriller.</p>

    <h2 style="font-size: 1.3rem; margin-top: 2rem; font-family: var(--font-serif);">The Name</h2>

    <p>Sinapeos is a fusion of <em>synapse</em> (the connection between neurons) and <em>eos</em> (dawn, in Greek). The dawn of new connections. New ways of seeing old problems. The moment the circuit completes and you understand something differently.</p>

    <h2 style="font-size: 1.3rem; margin-top: 2rem; font-family: var(--font-serif);">The Author</h2>

    <p>The lens I bring is not journalistic: it is psychological. I see why we click what we click, why certain stories grip us and others bounce off, and how to structure information so it lands in the way it was intended.</p>

    <p>The psych angle gives this project its edge. I understand why doom sells. And I understand how to deliver the same tension :  the same gripping uncertainty :  but with a resolution that leaves you feeling like you learned something real.</p>

    <h2 style="font-size: 1.3rem; margin-top: 2rem; font-family: var(--font-serif);">The Promise</h2>

    <p>Every story on this site is:</p>

    <ul>
      <li><strong>True.</strong> Every specific claim has at least two independent sources.</li>
      <li><strong>Earned.</strong> The solution had to be hard-won, not lucky. No easy answers here.</li>
      <li><strong>Honest.</strong> If the problem is still ongoing, we say so. If there are trade-offs, we name them.</li>
      <li><strong>Cinematic.</strong> Told as a story, not a Wikipedia article. You will remember it.</li>
    </ul>

    <p style="margin-top: 2rem; font-style: italic; color: var(--text-dim);">These are the fix we did not know we needed.</p>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>